  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Munjor, Kansas was founded in 1876 by Volga German immigrants who left Russia seeking new farmland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munjor%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Both villages originally called Monjou confused officials, so Russian authorities renamed the northern colony Obermonjou and the southern Neidermonjou.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munjor%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After arriving in New York on August 3, settlers rushed west to Topeka, then Herzog, Kansas, before settling 2 months later in Wheatland Township, now Munjor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munjor%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early Munjor settlers burned straw, sunflowers, and wood for fuel, and when those ran out, they used cattle manure called 'Mistholz'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.volgagerman.net/munjor",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After finding their first site unsatisfactory, settlers moved two months later to Section 25, Range 18 in Wheatland Township, the present site of Munjor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.volgagerman.net/munjor",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The largest wave of settlers left Saratov on July 8, 1876, bringing 108 families to what would become Munjor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munjor%2C_Kansas",
